{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 42, :comments ["<b>And Novach</b> was of the sons of Menashe." "<b>And he called it</b> Novach — Kenat. <b>לה</b> is defective (raphe), for he did not call it Novach permanently but only temporarily, until his return from across the Jordan, to make known that it was his. Here Rashi explained, and I wonder what he would interpret in two similar instances: ״ויאמר לה בועז״ (Boaz said to her) and ״לבנות לה בית״ (to build her a house). And some give a reason for them: ״ויאמר לה בועז״ (Boaz said to her) refers back to what she said — ״אנכי לא אהיה כאחת שפחותיך״ (I shall not be as one of your bondwomen). Boaz said to her — heaven forbid — neither as a mother nor as a bondwoman; this is what the elders said to Boaz: ״יתן ה׳ את האשה הבאה אל ביתך כרחל וכלאה״ (May HaShem make the woman who is coming into your house like Rachel and like Leah), who were mistresses. And what is written ״לבנות לה בית״ (to build her a house) — he sought to build but did not build: this is the poverty of Torah, that it went down to Babylonia — and whoever increases, increases forever; for they merited to learn but not to teach. And this is what our Sages said: ״לבנות ולא בנה״ (to build, and did not build). Therefore all three instances of לה are defective (raphe), for they are in place of לא (lo). <b>The order of the portion of Matot is complete.</b>"], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:20:35.379951231Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}